# team/config/operator_verbs.manifest
#
# Single source of truth for the operator verb set: every verb an operator
# can invoke, its script, its declared flag vocabulary, whether it mutates
# state, its idempotence class, its REST twin (or, on a planned row only,
# an explicit marker that no twin exists yet), its read|action enforcement
# class, a reference to its REST twin's response schema, and whether the
# REST twin and the CLI share one Python function. Before this
# file, the verb set was asserted in four places that drifted
# independently: each script's own OPERATOR_VALID_FLAGS array, the
# hardcoded _API_PARITY_MAP inside lint_api_parity.py, the per-command
# sections of docs/operator-commands.md, and the routes declared in the API
# routers. This file replaces the first two as the source of truth;
# team/scripts/lint_operator_verb_manifest.py is the reconciliation check
# that keeps it honest against the other two (the scripts themselves and
# the routers) every run.  docs/operator-commands.md is prose for humans,
# not machine-checked against this file -- keeping that document in sync
# with this manifest's rows is a manual documentation-maintenance step,
# not a mechanical one.
#
# Readers: team/scripts/lib/operator_verb_manifest.sh (bash) and
# team/pgai_agent_kanban/lib/operator_verb_manifest.py (Python) are the ONLY
# two places that parse this file. Every other consumer -- the
# reconciliation lint, lint_api_parity.py, the test suite -- imports/sources
# one of those two readers instead of re-parsing this file a second time.
#
# Parse rule: a line is a DATA line only when its first tab-delimited field
# is exactly "V". Every other line -- blank, or starting with anything else,
# including this header block -- is a comment and MUST be ignored by
# readers. '#' is used for comments here purely by convention (matching
# team/config/dashboard_windows.census); readers must not special-case '#'
# itself, only the "first field is V" rule.
#
# V (verb) record -- 12 fields after the sigil:
#   V  <verb>  <status>  <script>  <flags>  <mutates>  <idempotence>
#      <rest_method>  <rest_route>  <rest_body_model>  <class>  <schema_ref>
#      <impl>
#
#   verb            The verb name an operator types (e.g. "halt").
#
#   status          "active" -- the script named in <script> exists in the
#                   tree today, and the row declares its REST twin: every
#                   REST-describing column (<rest_method> through
#                   <schema_ref>) carries a real value. An active row with
#                   "ABSENT" in <rest_method>/<rest_route> is refused by
#                   lint_api_parity.py (its verb_missing_route class): a
#                   script an operator can run that no HTTP caller can
#                   reach is the drift this census exists to prevent.
#                   "planned" -- this verb belongs to the operator verb
#                   set but no script exists yet; see that verb's own
#                   notes below. A "planned" row carries
#                   "-" in every column from <script> through <idempotence>
#                   -- there is nothing yet to describe -- and "ABSENT" in
#                   every REST-describing column (<rest_method> through
#                   <schema_ref>). A "planned" row is the ONLY row that may
#                   carry ABSENT REST columns: with no script yet there is
#                   nothing to twin. A reader deciding "does this verb have
#                   a REST twin" still reads the REST columns alone without
#                   special-casing status first; only the script-
#                   describing columns use "-" to mark "no script yet" as
#                   a distinct condition from "no REST twin yet". <class>
#                   itself is a script-describing column (a verb's
#                   read|action bucket is meaningless before the verb's
#                   own behaviour exists to classify) and so stays "-" like
#                   its neighbours through <idempotence>.
#                   The reconciliation check never requires a "planned"
#                   verb's script to exist; it only requires the row to
#                   flip to "active" with real values once the verb ships.
#
#   script          Path to the verb's script, relative to team/ (e.g.
#                   "scripts/halt.sh"). Dashboard-internal scripts that are
#                   still operator-invocable live under scripts/dashboard/
#                   (e.g. "scripts/dashboard/show-queues.sh"). "-" for a
#                   planned verb.
#
#   flags           The verb's declared flag vocabulary, pipe-separated, in
#                   the order the script itself declares them. For a script
#                   using the shared OPERATOR_VALID_FLAGS=(...) array
#                   (team/scripts/lib/operator_args.sh), this is that array
#                   verbatim, including both "help" and "h" when a script
#                   lists both. Four scripts in this file (ship-rc.sh,
#                   scripts/dashboard/show-metrics.sh, scripts/dashboard/
#                   agent-metrics.sh, scripts/cm/cancel-rc.sh) parse their
#                   own arguments by hand instead of declaring
#                   OPERATOR_VALID_FLAGS; the reconciliation check trusts
#                   this column for each of them rather than re-deriving
#                   it, since there is no declarative array to diff
#                   against (team/pgai_agent_kanban/lib/
#                   operator_verb_manifest.py's NO_OPERATOR_VALID_FLAGS_
#                   ARRAY names all four). verify-rc-state.sh and
#                   scripts/dashboard/show-queues.sh both declare
#                   OPERATOR_VALID_FLAGS and are cross-checked like every
#                   other "active"
#                   row against the script's own array on every
#                   reconciliation run. "-" for a planned verb.
#
#   mutates         "yes" if invoking the verb can change on-disk or
#                   in-process state; "no" for a read-only verb. "-" for a
#                   planned verb.
#
#   idempotence     "idempotent" -- re-running the verb with the same
#                   arguments after a successful run produces the same end
#                   state without erroring or duplicating an effect.
#                   "non-idempotent" -- a second run either refuses (e.g.
#                   intake.sh's no-clobber guard) or produces a new/
#                   different effect (e.g. wake-now.sh firing another wake,
#                   deep-verify.sh appending another ledger entry).
#                   "read-only" -- mutates=no; idempotence is not a
#                   meaningful axis for a verb that never changes state.
#                   "-" for a planned verb. This column is descriptive
#                   metadata carried over from a targeted reading of each
#                   script's own comments and exit-code tables; a dedicated
#                   per-verb behavioural fixture matrix is the authoritative
#                   source for idempotence -- treat a mismatch there as the
#                   correction, not this column, as authoritative going
#                   forward.
#
#   rest_method     "GET" or "POST" -- every active verb has a REST twin.
#                   "ABSENT" only on a "planned" row, where no route fronts
#                   the verb because no script exists yet; an active row
#                   carrying ABSENT here fails lint_api_parity.py.
#
#   rest_route      The route path (e.g. "/operations/halt", "/show");
#                   "ABSENT" only on a "planned" row, exactly as for
#                   <rest_method>. "/operations/*"
#                   routes live in team/pgai_agent_kanban/api/routers/
#                   operations.py; bare routes (e.g. "/show", "/verdicts",
#                   "/metrics", "/agent-metrics") live in
#                   team/pgai_agent_kanban/api/reads.py.
#
#   rest_body_model The Pydantic request body model class name (e.g.
#                   "HaltBody") when the route both exists AND takes a JSON
#                   body whose fields should be held to script-flag parity
#                   by lint_api_parity.py. "NONE" when the route exists but
#                   has no fields meaningful to flag-parity checking --
#                   either it takes query parameters instead of a JSON body
#                   (GET /show, GET /verdicts, GET /metrics, GET
#                   /agent-metrics), or its body is accepted but
#                   deliberately ignored (HaltGlobalBody for halt-global /
#                   unhalt-global, which take no project or key). "ABSENT"
#                   when rest_method/rest_route are also "ABSENT" -- a
#                   "planned" row, never an active one.
#                   lint_api_parity.py's flag-to-body-field parity check
#                   runs only over rows where this column is a real class
#                   name -- "NONE" and "ABSENT" rows are excluded from that
#                   check by construction, matching the exact set the old
#                   hardcoded _API_PARITY_MAP covered (15 pairs).
#
#   class           "read" or "action" for an active verb -- the exact
#                   read|action vocabulary the REST-parity contract uses
#                   to split the verb estate
#                   into its two enforcement groups: a "read" verb's REST
#                   twin (once it exists) must return the SAME resolved
#                   data the CLI renders through one shared resolver, two
#                   renderers; an "action" verb's REST twin must accept the
#                   CLI's own parameters and enforce the SAME refusals
#                   dual-channel. Today this column agrees with <mutates>
#                   verb-for-verb (mutates=no <-> read, mutates=yes <->
#                   action) because every verb the estate carries today is
#                   cleanly one or the other, but the two columns are
#                   independently authored and answer different questions
#                   -- <mutates> is a fact about the SCRIPT's own on-disk
#                   effect, <class> is which parity-enforcement bucket the
#                   census places the verb in -- so a future verb whose
#                   parity treatment does not track its mutation fact has
#                   somewhere to say so without overloading <mutates>.
#                   "-" for a planned verb (see the <status> entry above).
#
#   schema_ref      A reference to the shape of the REST twin's JSON
#                   response body. "ShellEnvelope" names the standard
#                   ``{"exit_code": int, "stdout": str, "stderr": str,
#                   "warnings": list[str]}`` envelope every plain shell-out
#                   read route returns (team/pgai_agent_kanban/api/reads.py's
#                   own ``_make_envelope()``). "DualChannelShellEnvelope"
#                   names the same envelope EXTENDED on a refused call with
#                   the script's own dual-channel triple lifted out of
#                   stderr as top-level keys -- ``{"exit_code", "stdout",
#                   "stderr", "error", "message", "remedy", "warnings"}`` --
#                   the shape EVERY action route returns, because the
#                   action class requires the CLI's refusals to reach an
#                   HTTP caller as JSON error/message/remedy
#                   (routers/operations.py's
#                   ``_make_dual_channel_envelope()``; a script that refuses
#                   with plain stderr has that text lifted as ``message``
#                   under the route's stable ``<verb>.refused`` fallback
#                   key). An API-layer refusal on an action route (a
#                   missing required field, a confirmation not given)
#                   carries the same three keys at 422. The success body,
#                   including the hand-built "started" narration the
#                   detached-dispatch routes -- POST /operations/deep-verify,
#                   POST /operations/cold-build-now, POST /operations/
#                   wake-now's bg/all path -- return before their subprocess
#                   completes, is the plain four-key envelope. Its required
#                   key set is the UNION of the success and refusal shapes,
#                   like the read-verb unions below. A
#                   named class (e.g. "VerdictsResponse",
#                   "AgentMetricsResponse") identifies a route whose body is
#                   a custom, non-envelope shape documented in full in that
#                   route's own handler docstring in reads.py -- this
#                   column names the shape, it does not re-describe its
#                   fields a second time. "ABSENT" when rest_method/
#                   rest_route are also "ABSENT" (mirrors rest_body_model's
#                   own convention) -- that is, only on a "planned" row. Where
#                   a route offers a second, richer machine-readable
#                   variant behind a query flag (e.g. GET /metrics's
#                   ``?format=json`` mode returns a bare JSON array, not the
#                   envelope) this column records the route's PRIMARY
#                   contract -- the shape returned with no such flag
#                   supplied -- and the secondary shape is documented only
#                   in that route's own handler.
#
#   impl            How the REST twin and the CLI script reach the verb's
#                   behaviour. "module:<dotted.module.function>" (e.g.
#                   "module:pgai_agent_kanban.ops.read.get_release_state")
#                   -- the route calls that one Python function directly
#                   and starts no shell_out or subprocess on the way, and
#                   the CLI script reaches the SAME function through
#                   team/scripts/lib/pp_run_ops.sh (a "pp_run_ops <module>
#                   <function>" call, or a pp_pythonpath import of the
#                   function). "shell" -- every other row: the route shells
#                   to the script, or the two surfaces are not yet proven
#                   to share one function through pp_run_ops. "-" on a
#                   planned row. Both readers parse the column; the
#                   grammar has one implementation,
#                   operator_verb_manifest.parse_impl(), which
#                   lint_operator_verb_manifest.py uses to report a
#                   malformed or missing value.
#                   team/scripts/lint_api_route_impl.py verifies every
#                   "module:" row against the route and the script, and
#                   holds the number of "shell" rows to the baseline in
#                   team/config/api-route-impl-baseline.txt: the count may
#                   fall, never rise. A row flips from "shell" to
#                   "module:" in the same change that moves its route and
#                   CLI onto the shared function. A verb whose whole job
#                   is to start a process or lane (wake-now) stays
#                   "shell" and is listed, with its reason, in
#                   team/config/api-route-impl-launchers.txt; the lint
#                   reports it apart from the shell rows still to port.
#
# A note on "why": GET /verdicts calls resolve_verdicts in
# team/pgai_agent_kanban/ops/verdict.py in process -- the function
# scripts/why.sh (and its machine-readable sibling
# scripts/verdict-report.sh) reaches through pp_run_ops. This is the real
# REST twin for why, not merely an adjacent read verb: both surfaces
# resolve through the exact same function, so a dashboard and a terminal
# can never disagree about what a lane's verdict is (the parity
# guarantee docs/operator-commands.md's "why" section documents under "The
# same verdicts over HTTP"), and docs/operator-commands.md already
# documents GET /verdicts as why's published HTTP contract. why's REST
# columns below point at GET /verdicts / NONE (no body -- query params
# only) rather than staying ABSENT; its schema_ref is "VerdictsResponse",
# not "ShellEnvelope" -- the endpoint's body is {"verdicts": [...],
# "warnings": [...]}, never exit_code/stdout/stderr. ?details=true extends
# the same twin to why.sh --details' full diagnostic pane: the response
# gains "posture", "release_locks", and "queue_tails", rendered from the
# same resolved details why.sh --details' text sections are -- never a
# second derivation of lock provenance, queue state, or deferral
# posture. VerdictsResponse's required key set is therefore the UNION of
# both shapes (see team/scripts/lint_api_parity.py's own comment on that
# constant), not either shape alone.
#
# A note on rc-metrics and agent-metrics: both verbs already had a working
# REST twin (GET /metrics, GET /agent-metrics in team/pgai_agent_kanban/
# api/reads.py) before this row existed -- the API shipped ahead of the
# census that is supposed to be the single source of truth for the verb
# estate. The census's row-completeness rule (every operator verb the
# estate exposes gets a row) is what closes that gap; no route changed to
# add these two rows. agent-metrics.sh's
# response is a custom object, not the shell envelope, so its schema_ref
# is "AgentMetricsResponse"; rc-metrics' default (non-``?format=json``)
# response is the four-key envelope around the stdout show-metrics.sh
# prints, so its schema_ref is "ShellEnvelope". Both rows are "module:"
# rows: GET /metrics calls pgai_agent_kanban.lib.metrics.show_metrics in
# process and show-metrics.sh reaches it through pp_run_ops; GET
# /agent-metrics calls compute_agent_averages (the averaging function of
# pgai_agent_kanban.lib.agent_metrics, re-exported by
# pgai_agent_kanban.lib.metrics) and agent-metrics.sh reaches it through
# pp_run_ops pgai_agent_kanban.lib.metrics compute_agent_averages, whose
# renderer calls it for every project block.
#
# A note on release-state and deep-verify-status: both are read verbs of
# the REST-parity contract (release-state.md's four fields; the deep-verify
# ledger's entries and current status). Their CLI scripts,
# scripts/release-state.sh and scripts/deep-verify-status.sh, are each a
# thin renderer over the exact function its REST twin calls
# (pgai_agent_kanban.ops.read.get_release_state / get_deep_verify_status)
# -- one resolver, two renderers, never a second derivation. Both rows are
# "active" with real values.
#
# A note on the action rows: every one of them declares
# "DualChannelShellEnvelope" -- the action class's own contract is that the
# REST twin enforces the CLI's refusals dual-channel, so a route returning
# the plain four-key envelope on a refusal is the drift the parity gate's
# schema check reports. Four rows earned particular notes. halt-after's
# twin lifts the script's own --until refusals (halt-after.until_malformed,
# halt-after.until_scope_mismatch) to top-level keys so the action-parity
# fixtures can assert the same machine key over HTTP and CLI. cold-build-now.sh
# is a named alias that execs deep-verify.sh --project NAME, so POST
# /operations/cold-build-now takes deep-verify's detached dispatch (returns
# once the child starts, never with a verdict). cancel-rc's function,
# pgai_agent_kanban.cm.cancel.cancel_rc, which scripts/cm/cancel-rc.sh and
# POST /operations/cancel-rc share, refuses with plain stderr lines rather
# than the dual-channel emitter, so its twin surfaces that text as "message"
# under the stable fallback key "cancel_rc.refused"; its interactive
# "Proceed?" prompt has no HTTP equivalent, so the body's "yes" field is
# the confirmation and the route refuses 422 without it. Its "force" field
# (--force) is a separate parameter that bypasses only the Active-RC
# mismatch check and never confirms. release-locks has TWO REST surfaces for its two
# modes: this row names the verb's mutating --release/--force mode, POST
# /operations/release-locks, whose body model is what the flag-to-body
# parity check holds to the script's flags; the read-only listing mode (no
# --release) is GET /release-locks in reads.py, which stays registered in
# team/config/operator_verb_census_exempt.txt as the documented second
# route of an already-censused script rather than a second census row --
# this file carries exactly one REST twin per verb. The row's impl names
# pgai_agent_kanban.ops.locks.release_lock, the function POST
# /operations/release-locks and release-locks.sh --release share; the
# listing mode's GET route and the script's no-flag run share that
# module's list_locks the same way.
#
# Reading in bash: source team/scripts/lib/operator_verb_manifest.sh and
# call its ovm_* functions. Reading in Python: import
# pgai_agent_kanban.lib.operator_verb_manifest and call load_manifest() or
# get_verb(). Do not hand-parse this file a second time in either language.

V	halt	active	scripts/halt.sh	project|help	yes	idempotent	POST	/operations/halt	HaltBody	action	DualChannelShellEnvelope	module:pgai_agent_kanban.ops.write.halt
V	unhalt	active	scripts/unhalt.sh	project|help	yes	idempotent	POST	/operations/unhalt	HaltBody	action	DualChannelShellEnvelope	module:pgai_agent_kanban.ops.write.unhalt
V	halt-global	active	scripts/halt-global.sh	help	yes	idempotent	POST	/operations/halt-global	NONE	action	DualChannelShellEnvelope	module:pgai_agent_kanban.ops.write.halt_global
